From e706c940b298d8cbe46c30b9ba6d3306fd3daed8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 02:15:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] File a saved design under the account that saved it, not the
 URL's storefront
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A signed-in person's new design was written with the storefront id the request
resolved to, which the browser derives from the URL. Saving while a storefront
page was open therefore filed the plan into that store: it showed that store's
brand, appeared in that store's CRM, and was missing from the person's own
"My Designs" list on every other device. Re-opening it from another store's
link then failed the store-scope check — and the assisted read returned null for
the design's own owner when the host was pinned elsewhere — so the plan looked
locked to a store it did not belong to.

- New server/lib/designStoreOwnership.ts holds the two rules. An EXISTING
  design's own affiliateId decides its store for every read, write, rename,
  delete and open. A NEW design belongs to the signed-in account unless the
  person is a storefront customer or a member of the store they are on.
- design.save applies it and returns affiliateId, affiliateSlug, storeName and
  storeReason, so the Designer can say which account the plan went to.
- design.autosave, design.rename, design.remove and the owner branch of
  design.byId resolve the stored row's own store instead of the request's
  affiliateSlug. byId answers the owner before checking the pinned host.
- design.myDesigns is the account's whole list (every store), each row carrying
  its owning store's slug and name so a link opens it in the right place.
- customerAccount.overview and affiliateCustomers.get return the owning store
  with each design, so a CRM link is built from the design, not the open board.

Tests: server/lib/designStoreOwnership.test.ts (9) covers the rules;
server/routers/design.ownerScope.wiring.test.ts (10) pins where each procedure
takes its scope from.
